% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 5, section 5.7 ("Precise suppression-task rule with exceptions").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% The book's "she" is generalized to *a student*. The default rule says a student
% studies late in the library if she has an essay to write, UNLESS she is prevented
% (negation as failure). She is prevented when the library is not open, or she is
% unwell, has a more important meeting, or has been distracted -- the precise list
% of exceptions that suppress the conclusion (the Wason suppression task).

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*a student* has an essay to write.
*a student* is unwell; undefined.
*a student* has a more important meeting; undefined.
*a student* has been distracted; undefined.
the library is open.
*a student* is prevented from studying late in the library.
*a student* studies late in the library.

the knowledge base library study includes:

a student is prevented from studying late in the library
    if it is not the case that
        the library is open.

a student is prevented from studying late in the library
    if the student is unwell.

a student is prevented from studying late in the library
    if the student has a more important meeting.

a student is prevented from studying late in the library
    if the student has been distracted.

a student studies late in the library
    if the student has an essay to write
    and it is not the case that
        the student is prevented from studying late in the library.

scenario normal is:
    sue has an essay to write.
    the library is open.
    study expects answers ["sue studies late in the library"].

scenario closed is:
    sue has an essay to write.
    study expects answers [].

scenario unwell is:
    sue has an essay to write.
    the library is open.
    sue is unwell.
    study expects answers [].

query study is:
    which student studies late in the library.
